<header><p class="st-kicker">princely state · Indian princely states</p><h1 class="st-title">Bilaspur State</h1><p class="st-meta">697 CE – 1954 CE (approx.) · capital Bilaspur</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The Chandela Rajput hill state of Kahlur on the Sutlej, a rival of Guru Gobind Singh and briefly a separate state of the Indian Union before joining Himachal Pradesh.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Tradition dates Kahlur to 697, when Bir Chand, a Chandela <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajputs">Rajput</a> from central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, carved out a domain on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sutlej-river">Sutlej</a> in the lower <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/himalayas">Himalaya</a>. The town of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bilaspur-himachal-pradesh">Bilaspur</a>, which gave the state its later name, was founded as the capital in the mid-17th century.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Kahlur was one of the leading <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shimla">Shimla</a> hill states, ruled by Rajas who held land through local <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajputs">Rajput</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahmins">Brahmin</a> families. Raja <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bhim-chand-of-kahlur">Bhim Chand</a> fought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guru-gobind-singh">Guru Gobind Singh</a> in the 1680s and 1690s, and the state was later overrun by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gurkhas">Gurkhas</a> of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-nepal">Kingdom of Nepal</a>. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-nepalese-war">Anglo-Nepalese War</a> of 1814–1816 the British restored the Raja under a sanad.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Raja Anand Chand kept <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bilaspur-himachal-pradesh">Bilaspur</a> apart after 1947, and it was run as a separate centrally administered state until it joined <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/himachal-pradesh">Himachal Pradesh</a> in 1954. The old town of Bilaspur was submerged in the 1960s by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gobind-sagar">Gobind Sagar</a> reservoir of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bhakra-dam">Bhakra Dam</a>, and a new town was built above it.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/bilaspur-state.jpg" alt="Bilaspur State" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: Robert Alfers · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bilaspur_flag.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>697 CE – 1954 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>princely state</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Bilaspur</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Bhim Chand, Anand Chand</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Hinduism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Kahluri</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>India</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Kahlur</dd></dl></aside></div>

